  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Days in East Africa start early, with the sun at six, and the weather is mostly a question of rain or no rain.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Leo ni Jumatatu.", "base": "Today is Monday." },
      { "target": "Ninaamka saa kumi na mbili.", "base": "I wake up at six (the twelfth hour)." },
      { "target": "Ninafanya kazi kila siku.", "base": "I work every day." },
      { "target": "Ninapenda kucheza mpira.", "base": "I like playing football." },
      { "target": "Mvua inanyesha.", "base": "It’s raining." },
      { "target": "Kuna joto sana leo.", "base": "It’s very hot today." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "days",
        "title": "Counting the days",
        "body": [
          "Most days are numbered from Saturday, the first day of the old Swahili week: **Jumamosi** (day one), **Jumapili** (day two), **Jumatatu** (three), **Jumanne** (four), **Jumatano** (five).",
          "**Alhamisi** (Thursday) and **Ijumaa** (Friday, the day of Friday prayers) come from Arabic."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "verb-to",
        "title": "Ku- means “to”",
        "body": [
          "The dictionary form of a verb starts with **ku-**: **kucheza** (to play), **kusoma** (to read, study), **kupika** (to cook), **kulala** (to sleep). Take off **ku-** and add **nina-** to say “I am …ing”: **ninapika**, **ninalala**.",
          "After **ninapenda** (I like), keep the **ku-** form: **Ninapenda kucheza** (I like playing)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "weather-kuna",
        "title": "Weather with kuna",
        "body": [
          "Use **kuna** for the weather: **Kuna joto** (it’s hot), **Kuna baridi** (it’s cold), **Kuna jua** (it’s sunny). Rain “falls”: **Mvua inanyesha**."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Two rainy seasons",
        "body": [
          "Much of East Africa has the long rains, **masika**, around March to May, and the short rains, **vuli**, around October to December. Farmers plant by them, and a good rain is welcomed as a blessing."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Football everywhere",
        "body": [
          "**Mpira** (football) is played on every patch of open ground, and the big local derbies, like Simba against Yanga in Dar es Salaam, bring cities to a stop."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
